"""Topics and explanations for CPRITE 2023 Q51-100."""

META: dict[int, tuple[str, str]] = {
    51: ("Assessment",
        "Daytime sleepiness, hypnopompic hallucinations, and short REM latency on polysomnography are the narcolepsy cluster; the confirmatory next test is the multiple sleep latency test (mean latency and sleep-onset REM periods). Cerebrospinal fluid hypocretin is more invasive and is reserved for equivocal studies. HLA typing is associated, not diagnostic. EEG evaluates seizures, and serum iron evaluates restless legs. Teaching point: After a suggestive PSG, confirm narcolepsy with MSLT, not CSF orexin first."),
    52: ("Ethics & Forensics",
        "A child's dissent to nontherapeutic research must be honored even when a parent consents. Acknowledge the refusal and do not enroll. This parenting-intervention study is not a life-saving exception that would justify overriding dissent. Do not pressure the child, send the family home to wear him down, enroll because 'the parent is the real subject,' or treat oppositionality as decision-making incapacity. Teaching point: Child says no to research -> stop; parent consent does not override dissent."),
    53: ("Psychopharmacology",
        "Pediatric OCD with incomplete response to exposure and response prevention plus a maximally tolerated SSRI is classically augmented with risperidone. Adding a second SSRI (sertraline) is not augmentation. Melatonin, valproate, and cyproheptadine have no OCD evidence here. Later practice sometimes prefers aripiprazole or a clomipramine switch, but the board key remains risperidone. Teaching point: Child OCD partial response on SSRI plus ERP -> risperidone augmentation."),
    54: ("Systems & Prevention",
        "Current Procedural Terminology codes name the specific service delivered in the session (E/M, psychotherapy, etc.). Diagnosis lives in ICD codes. Time, payer, and clinician training may modify a code but are not what CPT itself records. Teaching point: CPT = what you did; ICD = why you did it."),
    55: ("Development",
        "A high-functioning 14-year-old trying on sexual-orientation labels and career plans, without gender dysphoria, is in normal adolescent identity exploration, which is often fluid until later development. It does not predict adult homosexual or transgender identity, flag a depressive disorder, or mean a personality disturbance that needs treatment. Teaching point: Teen sexuality and identity exploration are often fluid and are not a diagnosis."),
    56: ("Psychopharmacology",
        "Carbamazepine (and other sodium-channel blockers) can exacerbate myoclonus in juvenile myoclonic epilepsy. Valproate, topiramate, and levetiracetam treat JME. Lamotrigine is the closest distractor because it can worsen myoclonus in a subset, but it remains an accepted JME option and is not the classic precipitant. Teaching point: JME plus carbamazepine = more myoclonus; prefer valproate or levetiracetam."),
    57: ("Development",
        "The visual-cliff paradigm in which a 10-month-old uses the caregiver's facial expression to decide whether to cross is social referencing. Joint attention is sharing gaze on an object. Object permanence and object constancy are cognitive/attachment constructs, and emotional refueling is Mahler's rapprochement behavior. Teaching point: Check caregiver's face, then act = social referencing."),
    58: ("Ethics & Forensics",
        "The most common route to a psychiatric data breach is employees opening malicious files (phishing attachments and malware), not paper charts, cloud hosting per se, or a shortage of IT staff. Training gaps contribute, but the proximal event the boards want is the clicked file. Teaching point: The usual HIPAA breach is an employee accessing a malicious file."),
    59: ("Systems & Prevention",
        "Lifelong learning (practice-based learning and improvement) is self-directed review of evidence-based literature about the presenting symptoms. Obtaining a consult, more labs, or an outpatient referral is clinical care. Forming a committee is systems quality work, not personal lifelong learning. Teaching point: Lifelong learning on the boards = go read the evidence yourself."),
    60: ("Neuroscience",
        "Affect incongruent with content, explosive dysarthria, a brisk gag with coughing/crying, and dysphagia with a normal oropharynx is pseudobulbar (upper-motor corticobulbar) palsy, often with pseudobulbar affect. True bulbar palsy is lower-motor: wasted tongue, absent gag, no emotional incontinence. Myasthenia is fatigable without this reflex pattern; conversion is not first when the neurologic exam is this specific. Teaching point: Labile affect plus brisk gag plus dysphagia = pseudobulbar palsy, not bulbar."),
    61: ("Assessment",
        "The Mood and Feelings Questionnaire is a validated self-report depression scale for children as young as eight. The PHQ-9 is for older adolescents/adults. The Young Mania Rating Scale is a mania interview, the Pediatric Symptom Checklist is a broadband screen, and the Spence scale is anxiety. Teaching point: Depression self-report at age 8 = Mood and Feelings Questionnaire."),
    62: ("Development",
        "Self-conscious emotions (shame, guilt, pride, embarrassment) emerge after self-recognition and awareness of standards, typically by age three. Six months and one year are too early (social smile, stranger anxiety). Five and seven years are well after the capacity is in place. Teaching point: Capacity for shame is typically present by age 3."),
    63: ("Systems & Prevention",
        "An electronic-record flag when BMI rises, feeding back to clinicians and the clinic manager, is continuous quality improvement: ongoing process monitoring with a feedback loop. Root-cause analysis follows a sentinel event. Consumer satisfaction, health-plan protocols, and isolated outcome measurement are different tools. Teaching point: Live chart flags that feed back to the team = continuous quality improvement."),
    64: ("Development",
        "Tanner 2 breasts and pubic hair at age 10 in a girl is normal puberty (thelarche typically 8-13; mean around 10). Overweight can nudge timing earlier but not into precocity. Premature adrenarche is pubic hair before 8 without breast development. CYP21 deficiency, a virilizing tumor, and glucocorticoid resistance produce more frank androgen excess, not isolated Tanner 2 at 10. Teaching point: 10-year-old girl, Tanner 2 = normal puberty, not precocious."),
    65: ("Ethics & Forensics",
        "A transplant-eligibility psychiatric exam creates dual agency: the evaluator is not solely the patient's advocate, so beneficence toward the identified patient is in tension with gatekeeping scarce organs. Autonomy, assent, confidentiality, and informed consent matter in the workup but are not the named dilemma of a listing recommendation. Teaching point: Transplant psych eval puts beneficence in conflict with gatekeeping; you are not a pure advocate."),
    66: ("Neuroscience",
        "Cortisol (glucocorticoids) decreases hippocampal neurogenesis. Oxytocin and progesterone tend to support it; glucose and glutamate are not the classic suppressor named here. Teaching point: Cortisol decreases hippocampal neurogenesis."),
    67: ("Assessment",
        "Forced meals followed by long bathroom trips suggest covert vomiting; an elevated serum (salivary) amylase supports that the patient is not accurately reporting purging. Phosphate tracks refeeding risk, not secret vomiting. Calcium, lipids, and AST do not detect purging. Teaching point: Meal then long bathroom time -> check amylase for vomiting."),
    68: ("Psychopathology",
        "An abused, recently placed 8-year-old with a suicidal threat, sadness, and voices saying he is 'no good,' who is still passing school and popular with peers, puts posttraumatic stress disorder high on the differential; children with PTSD can have trauma-themed hallucinations. Autism is unlikely given peer success. Separation anxiety, ODD, and OCD do not organize this trauma-plus-voices picture. Teaching point: Traumatized child with 'you're no good' voices -> put PTSD high, not automatically a primary psychotic disorder."),
    69: ("Psychopathology",
        "Before calling excessive gaming/spending an addictive disorder, a manic episode must be ruled out: spending sprees, dysphoria/irritability when the game is blocked, lying, and stealing can all be mania. Kleptomania, conduct disorder, depression, and OCD are relevant differentials but are not the DSM prerequisite the question is asking for. Teaching point: Spending sprees plus irritability when stopped -> rule out mania before 'addiction.'"),
    70: ("Neuroscience",
        "Histamine H1 receptors (tuberomammillary wake projections) are the primary listed regulators of arousal and the sleep-wake cycle; H1 blockade is why many antihistamines sedate. Muscarinic-1, dopamine-2, serotonin-1A, and beta-1 receptors are not the main sleep-wake switch. Teaching point: Arousal/sleep-wake receptor = histamine H1."),
    71: ("Consultation & Schools",
        "The Juvenile Justice and Delinquency Prevention Act requires public schools to count credits earned in the juvenile facility toward graduation. It does not, as the mandated next act, require a named transition plan, homebound online school, special-education testing, or alternative-program placement. Teaching point: Juvenile-facility credits must count toward a public-school diploma."),
    72: ("Development",
        "Youth with repeated foster placements commonly show premature/compulsive self-reliance: they stop depending on adults after serial losses. Secure attachment and academic achievement are the opposite of the typical pattern. Apathy and developmental regression can occur but are not the commonly demonstrated adaptation the boards want. Teaching point: Many foster moves -> self-reliant kids who stop asking adults for help."),
    73: ("Neuroscience",
        "Reciprocal inhibition between medial prefrontal cortex and amygdala (mPFC top-down braking of the amygdala) decreases the stress/fear response. Pons, cerebellum, corpus callosum, and medulla are not that circuit. Teaching point: mPFC brakes the amygdala -> less stress."),
    74: ("Research Methods",
        "Two groups compared on a continuous mean (HAM-D scores) is a t-test. Chi-square needs categorical counts. Logistic regression needs a binary outcome. A correlation coefficient tests association, not a two-group mean difference. Mixed-factorial ANOVA is for extra factors or repeated measures the stem did not give. Teaching point: Two-group mean difference = t-test."),
    75: ("Development",
        "A 12-year-old dying of cancer can mourn a lost future self ('I will never be able to drive a car'). A 6-year-old more often uses magical reunion, angels, and a need to keep parents nearby. Appetite loss is nonspecific across ages. Teaching point: Older dying children name lost future selves; younger ones stay with separation and magic."),
    76: ("Neuroscience",
        "The most replicated first-year MRI phenotype in autism is average (normal) brain size at birth followed by accelerated growth. Increased or decreased size at birth, with or without later undergrowth, is not the replicated pattern. Teaching point: ASD brains start average, then grow too fast in year one."),
    77: ("Assessment",
        "The correct probe of a child's family drawing is the open-ended 'Tell me about your drawing.' Do not interpret the missing mother, praise the tree, rate the child's performance, or ask for more drawings to dodge the content. Teaching point: Family drawing -> 'Tell me about it,' not a comment on who is missing."),
    78: ("Psychopharmacology",
        "A 10-year-old with days of sleeplessness, grandiosity, and a bipolar pedigree has acute mania; risperidone has pediatric RCT evidence and FDA approval for mania ages 10-17. Lithium is approved at 12 and older, so this child is too young for that label. Lamotrigine is not an acute-mania drug. Valproate is off-label with weaker pediatric data. Oxcarbazepine failed a pediatric bipolar trial. Teaching point: 10-year-old acute mania -> risperidone, not lithium."),
    79: ("Consultation & Schools",
        "Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act applies to programs that receive federal funds. A private school that does not can legally refuse to honor a 504 plan on that basis. That the plan originated in a public school, diagnostic labeling, impairment severity, and intervention cost are not the statutory hook. Teaching point: No federal dollars -> no 504 obligation."),
    80: ("Assessment",
        "When sleep studies are equivocal, CSF orexin/hypocretin below 110 pg/mL is diagnostic of narcolepsy (type 1). Restless legs, REM sleep behavior disorder, and circadian phase disorders do not have this biomarker. Teaching point: Equivocal PSG/MSLT plus orexin <110 pg/mL = narcolepsy."),
    81: ("Development",
        "Pointing to a desired object (protoimperative pointing) appears as early as 7-9 months and is the motor act that recruits a preferred adult, thereby establishing social preference and early joint engagement. Imitation, cooing, visual tracking, and eye contact are earlier or different systems and are not the motor skill this item is after. Teaching point: Early pointing to what they want is the motor root of social preference."),
    82: ("Development",
        "Synaptic pruning and myelination between 13 and 25 years most improve prefrontal executive function: solving problems. Distinguishing textures, memorizing vocabulary, spatial orientation, and precise movements ride earlier sensory, language, parietal, and motor curves. Teaching point: Pruning and myelination at 13-25 -> better problem-solving, not better vocabulary."),
    83: ("Psychotherapy",
        "Encouraging talk, accurate empathy, titrating affect to what the patient can bear, and not uncovering repressed material is supportive psychotherapy. Interpersonal therapy maps a role dispute or loss. Habit reversal is for tics/body-focused repetition. Psychodynamic work would pursue the repressed. DBT adds skills and dialectics. Teaching point: Empathy and strengthening coping without uncovering = supportive therapy."),
    84: ("Neuroscience",
        "Premenstrual affective symptoms reflect an abnormal CNS response to typical luteal estrogen/progesterone fluctuations, not abnormally low estrogen, high progesterone, or a missing hormonal link. GnRH-agonist plus add-back studies established that hormone levels are normal; the brain's response is not. Teaching point: Premenstrual mood symptoms = abnormal response to normal hormones."),
    85: ("Psychopharmacology",
        "A lithium level of 2.5 mEq/L with confusion and gastrointestinal toxicity after an OTC cold/pain medicine is from naproxen: NSAIDs reduce lithium clearance. Acetaminophen is the safer OTC analgesic with lithium. Guaifenesin, pseudoephedrine, and diphenhydramine do not cause this jump. Teaching point: Lithium plus naproxen (NSAID) -> toxic lithium; acetaminophen is the safer fever drug."),
    86: ("Assessment",
        "Asking the child to draw a picture of themselves is a projective technique (Draw-A-Person). Clarifying with a parent, building with blocks, and observing parent or stranger interaction are interview, developmental, or observational methods, not projective. Teaching point: 'Draw yourself' is projective; block-building is not."),
    87: ("Neuroscience",
        "Prolactin is under direct tonic inhibition by tuberoinfundibular dopaminergic (TIDA) neurons; D2 blockade raises prolactin. GABAergic, cholinergic, serotonergic, and glutamatergic neurons are not the direct hypothalamic brake named here. Teaching point: Prolactin is under dopaminergic inhibition from the TIDA tract."),
    88: ("Systems & Prevention",
        "The Mental Health Parity and Addiction Equity Act addresses insurers managing and reimbursing mental illness more restrictively than medical/surgical care. The Affordable Care Act expanded coverage, the ADA is disability discrimination, and the Children's Health Act and Garrett Lee Smith Act are other federal programs, not parity of insurance design. Teaching point: Unequal mental-health insurance rules -> Mental Health Parity and Addiction Equity Act."),
    89: ("Psychopharmacology",
        "Days of mutism, posturing, unresponsiveness, and rigidity with normal vitals and no fever is catatonia; first-line treatment with supportive care is lorazepam. Risperidone can worsen catatonia. Bromocriptine is for NMS, cyproheptadine for serotonin syndrome, and ECT is next if benzodiazepines fail. Teaching point: Kid in a rigid stare -> lorazepam for catatonia, not a neuroleptic."),
    90: ("Ethics & Forensics",
        "Advance care planning starts now by asking the child whether anyone has discussed wishes in the event of serious illness, even while oncology has not declared the illness life-limiting. Do not avoid death talk, wait for a definitive prognosis, outsource to the chaplain, or pick a surrogate with caregivers only. Teaching point: Advance care planning starts by asking the child what they already know and want."),
    91: ("Assessment",
        "The Pediatric Symptom Checklist is the broadband primary-care screen for childhood psychiatric risk. PHQ-9 and the Mood and Feelings Questionnaire are depression-specific, SCARED is anxiety-specific, and the McMaster device assesses family functioning. Teaching point: Primary-care general psych screen = Pediatric Symptom Checklist."),
    92: ("Development",
        "A school-age child concluding 'I'm not good like the other kids' after a missed goal is Erikson's industry versus inferiority. Initiative versus guilt is preschool, identity versus role confusion is adolescence, and trust/autonomy are infant and toddler stages. Teaching point: 'I'm not good like the other kids' = industry vs inferiority."),
    93: ("Psychotherapy",
        "The engagement-building first line is a motivational-interviewing affirmation: 'You must have put in lots of effort to stay away from drugs.' Asking which drugs, lecturing to stay in the program, and probing program history are assessment or advice and land worse as the opening move. Teaching point: First line to a proud stretch of sobriety = affirm the effort."),
    94: ("Psychotherapy",
        "The initial phase of interpersonal psychotherapy builds an inventory of the adolescent's relationships and identifies a problem area. Communication analysis, role-plays, problem-solving practice, and a mood thermometer belong to the middle phase or to CBT. Teaching point: Early IPT = relationship inventory, not communication analysis yet."),
    95: ("Psychopathology",
        "Assaulting a pharmacist to steal drugs and sell them for profit is instrumental (proactive, goal-directed) aggression. Reactive, affective, impulsive, and defensive aggression are hot, unplanned, and threat-driven, not a robbery for secondary gain. Teaching point: Aggression for profit = instrumental."),
    96: ("Psychopathology",
        "Recurrent, sexually arousing fantasies about a prepubescent child for six months, with marked shame, in a 17-year-old at least five years older, meet pedophilic disorder (acted-on behavior is not required when distress is present). Enjoyable sexual arousal is not OCD. Fetishistic and voyeuristic disorders need objects or watching; conduct disorder needs antisocial acts, not fantasies alone. Teaching point: Persistent, arousing, distressing fantasies of a prepubescent child = pedophilic disorder."),
    97: ("Research Methods",
        "The forest plot's overall diamond sits just left of 1 on a Drug X-versus-placebo efficacy axis, but its confidence interval crosses the line of no effect, so there is no statistically significant difference: Drug X and placebo have the same likelihood of decreasing anxiety. Do not claim Drug X works because the point estimate favors it, and do not read the plot as adverse effects (those options are distractors; the axis is efficacy). Teaching point: If the pooled diamond's CI includes 1, Drug X is not significantly better than placebo."),
    98: ("Research Methods",
        "A five-fold increase in a rare side effect is a relative-risk statement that can still be a tiny absolute risk; that distinction (absolute risk versus relative risk reduction) is what families need. Sensitivity/specificity, reliability, cohort-versus-case-control, and modifiable-versus-nonmodifiable risks are not the interpretation problem in this stem. Teaching point: Relative 'five times' of a rare side effect still needs the absolute risk."),
    99: ("Assessment",
        "Nocturnal snorting/coughing, bruxism, daytime fatigue, and afternoon crankiness in a 5-year-old is obstructive sleep apnea until proven otherwise; overnight polysomnography is the diagnostic test. Actigraphy maps rest-activity, not apnea. Chest radiograph, nasal endoscopy, and a dental exam may find contributors but do not diagnose sleep-disordered breathing. Teaching point: Snoring plus daytime crankiness -> polysomnography for OSA."),
    100: ("Psychopharmacology",
        "Unwanted, ego-dystonic thoughts of throwing the baby out a window, with window avoidance and no psychosis, are postpartum OCD, not postpartum psychosis. Fluoxetine 40 mg is an incomplete OCD dose after only partial benefit, so the next pharmacologic step is to increase fluoxetine. Do not start a neuroleptic, a benzodiazepine, or a mood stabilizer, and do not switch antidepressants yet. Teaching point: Postpartum window-avoidant harm thoughts without psychosis -> push the SSRI, not an antipsychotic."),
}
